``` Introduction

`@cd <Type or instance>` pushes that scope directly onto the stack, so declarations made inside it become real members of the target -- unlike sibling scopes (see learn/sibling_scopes.ore), which only add a fallback lookup place without mutating anything. `@cd ..` pops back out to whatever scope was active before.
```
Button {
	label := 'default'
}

@cd Button
	css_filter := 'invert()'
@cd ..

b := Button()
@puts "b.css_filter: `b.css_filter`"   # b.css_filter: invert()


``` Reopening a Type extends every instance

`css_filter` above was added to the `Button` type, not to any one instance -- so every `Button()` built afterward (and even ones already built) sees it, the same as if it had been declared in the original body:
```
another_button := Button()
@puts "another_button.css_filter: `another_button.css_filter`"   # another_button.css_filter: invert() -- same declaration, shared by every Button


``` Reopening one instance changes just that instance

`@cd` also accepts an instance directly, in which case the new declarations land only on that one value -- every other instance of the same type is untouched:
```
c := Button()
d := Button()

@cd c
	onclick := 'clicked'
@cd ..

@puts "c.onclick: `c.onclick`"   # c.onclick: clicked
# d.onclick would raise Ore::Undeclared_Identifier -- only c was reopened, not the Button type
